      "message": "### 1. The `_res` Epidemic (Across `updater.cc` and `cli.cc`)\n\n**The Issue: Abbreviated Wrapper Variables**\nBecause this codebase heavily relies on C++23 `std::expected` for error handling, the author needed names for the wrapper objects before extracting the underlying values. They defaulted to appending `_res` (for \"result\") to almost everything: `fw_data_res`, `map_res`, `version_res`, `erase_res`, `page_res`, `io_res`, `exec_res`.\n\nGoogle C++ Style dictates avoiding abbreviations. Furthermore, naming a variable just \"result\" doesn\u0027t describe the data it holds.\n\n**Current (in `UpdateFw`):**\n\n```cpp\n  const auto map_res \u003d GetMemoryMap(current_mode);\n  // ...\n  const auto version_res \u003d GetBootVersion(device, current_mode);\n  // ...\n  const auto download_res \u003d DownloadBin(device, fw_data, target_addr);\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:** Spell out `_result` or, even better, name the variable based on the action being attempted.\n\n```cpp\n  const auto memory_map_result \u003d GetMemoryMap(current_mode);\n  // ...\n  const auto version_check_result \u003d GetBootVersion(device, current_mode);\n  // ...\n  const auto download_status \u003d DownloadBin(device, fw_data, config_addr);\n\n```\n\n### 2. `updater.cc` - `ErasePage`\n\n**The Issue: Plural Noun for a Scalar Count (`pages`)**\nIn English and programming, a plural noun like `pages` implies a collection, array, or container (e.g., `std::vector\u003cPage\u003e pages`). Here, it is storing a single `uint32_t` representing the total *number* of pages to erase.\n\n**Current:**\n\n```cpp\n  const uint32_t pages \u003d\n      static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(DivRoundUp(length, kFocalConfigSize));\n      \n  return SendBulkDataOut(device,\n                         CmdFormat{\n                             // ...\n                             .length \u003d ToLittleEndian(pages),\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:** Use the `_count` suffix to instantly tell the reader this is a mathematical scalar.\n\n```cpp\n  const uint32_t page_count \u003d\n      static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(DivRoundUp(length, kFocalConfigSize));\n\n```\n\n### 3. `updater.cc` - `GetBootVersion`\n\n**The Issue: Misleading Type Implication (`null_pos`)**\nThe variable `null_pos` implies a \"position\" (typically an integer index like `size_t` returned by `std::string::find`). However, `std::ranges::find` returns an **iterator**. Naming an iterator `_pos` creates a subtle cognitive mismatch for modern C++ developers who expect iterators to be named with an `_it` suffix or contextually like `end`.\n\n**Current:**\n\n```cpp\n  const auto null_pos \u003d std::ranges::find(data, \u0027\\0\u0027);\n  const std::string version(data.begin(), null_pos);\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:** Clarify that this is an iterator marking the end of the string.\n\n```cpp\n  const auto null_terminator_it \u003d std::ranges::find(data, \u0027\\0\u0027);\n  const std::string version(data.begin(), null_terminator_it);\n\n```\n\n### 4. `libusb_transport.cc` - Factory and Context Management\n\n**The Issue: Ubiquitous C-style Abbreviations (`ctx`, `dev`)**\nIn the C world of `libusb`, variables are always `ctx` and `dev`. However, in C++, we have the namespace to be expressive. The Google C++ Style Guide explicitly states: *\"Do not abbreviate by deleting letters within a word.\"*\n\n**Current:**\n\n```cpp\n  libusb_context* raw_ctx \u003d nullptr;\n  if (libusb_init(\u0026raw_ctx) !\u003d LIBUSB_SUCCESS) { ... }\n  ScopedContext ctx(raw_ctx, libusb_exit);\n\n  ScopedDeviceList dev_list(raw_list);\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:**\n\n```cpp\n  libusb_context* raw_context \u003d nullptr;\n  if (libusb_init(\u0026raw_context) !\u003d LIBUSB_SUCCESS) { ... }\n  ScopedContext context(raw_context, libusb_exit);\n\n  ScopedDeviceList device_list(raw_list);\n\n```\n\n### 5. `updater.h` - Write Protection Enum\n\n**The Issue: Awkward English (`kOutRange`)**\nThe final element of the `FlashWpArea` enum acts as a boundary marker for validation. `kOutRange` is grammatically awkward and misses a preposition.\n\n**Current:**\n\n```cpp\nenum class FlashWpArea : uint32_t {\n  // ...\n  kAll,   // Protect the entire flash (Bootloader, RO, and RW)\n  kOutRange,\n};\n\n```\n\n**Improvement:** Fix the grammar or use standard boundary naming (like `kCount` or `kMax`).\n\n```cpp\nenum class FlashWpArea : uint32_t {\n  // ...\n  kAll,   \n  kOutOfRange, // or kMaxValue\n};\n\n```",
